// This message file contains text to be used in game-supplied newspapers
// Only the message line numbers 1000 or higher are valid
// Each new newspaper begins on a multiple of 10, and can use up to 10
// lines, which must be consecutive. The first line is always the main 
// headline, while the 9 remaining lines go into 4 columns of the paper.
// Format: each line begins with a font number and justification letter,
// followed by the text for that line.

// Some font numbers to use:
//	490 Extra Large Headline
//	486 Large Headline
//	492 Normal Headline
//	483 Story Body
//	488 Clip Art 0-6

// Justification letters (you must use one)
//	l = left
//	c = center

// NOTE: The first two blocks, at 1000 and 1010, are reserved for UI use

{1000}{EXTRA!}
{1001}{[ THE TARANTIAN ]}
{1002}{Price 2 Coins}
{1003}{The Best Source of News-worthy Items in Arcanum}
{1004}{492c _ WEATHER _}
{1005}{483l The Meteorological Service Bureau of Tarant is Predicting sunny weather for the next Two to Three days, with an increasing Chance of Precipitation before the Passage of a Fortnight.}
{1006}{492c _ TAX INCREASE _}
{1007}{483l The Parliament is again considering Raising the Levy of Incomes in Tarant by another 2 coins for every 100 earned. The Speaker has refuted all Public Criticism of the Increase, by Citing the importance of Public Works projects currently underway.}
{1008}{492c _ PUBLIC SERVICE ANNOUNCEMENT _}
{1009}{483l There will be a City Council meeting this Friday which is Open to the Public. The Meeting will commence sharply at 7pm.}
{1010}{492c _ EDITORIAL REMINDER _}
{1011}{483l The Tarantian would like to take this Opportunity to remind its Gentle Reader that all letters to Editor do not necessarily Reflect the views of the Newspaper or its Owner, Sir William Willaby.}
{1012}{492c _ PROHIBITION _}
{1013}{483l The spectre of Prohibition has risen once again, as Parliament considers a Bill to ban the sales of Spirits in the confines of the City proper. Supporters of the Bill cite increased Crime and Work Absenteeism figures as the principle Show of Cause for the bill, while its Critics cite similiar Statistics during the last Prohibition era over 30 years ago.}
{1014}{492c _ UNIVERSITY COLLOQUIUM SERIES _}
{1015}{483l The Engineering School of the University of Tarant is holding a Distinguished Speaker Series this semester. Interested parties are invited to contact the University Registrar for more Information.}
{1016}{492c _ CHARITY BALL _}
{1017}{483l The Ladies Society of Tarant will once again hold a Charity Ball this Saturday evening. This Event will benefit Orphans and Survivors of Foreign Wars. Festivities will begin at 8pm. Strict dress code will be enforced.}
{1018}{492c _ TIDAL FIGURES _}
{1019}{483l High tides will occur at 6:15am and 7:30pm today, with low tides at approximately 1:05pm and 25 minutes after midnight. Full tidal charts can be found on page 8.}

//Game Related Newspapers
//Use lines 1000-4990
//Generic Newspapers Start at line 5000

{1020}{490c GARRINGSBURG HEIST!
PAINTING STOLEN!}
{1021}{492c _ THIEVES STILL AT LARGE _}
{1022}{483l Late on Tuesday evening, the mansion of Mr. James and Mrs. Evelyn Garringsburg, at 63 Devonshire Way, was broken into by thieves and ransacked, according to sources close to the family.  Among the many items stolen was the famed 'Kerghan and Persephone', painted by Pizarro.  Sold last year at Renaud's for a reputed 50,000 gold pieces, the painting was the show piece of the Garringsburg gallery.
Mr. Garringsburg, a long-standing member of the Wellington Gentlemen's Club, had this to say--"You can be assured that the perpetrators of this heinous crime will be brought to justice, and that reparations will be made."  Mrs. Garringburg was unavailable for comment.
The Garringsburgs have offered a 300 gold piece reward.  This crime is only the latest of many art-related robberies, which have included the "Lorek in repose" statue, and the Blendom Vase.}

{1030}{490c ELVEN TOMBS DISCOVERED!}
{1031}{492c _ DISCOVERY OF TOMBS IN THE MORBIHAN PLAINS BY LEARNED PROFESSOR R.I. JAMES CAUSE OF MUCH EXCITEMENT _}
{1032}{483l Professor William G. Harriman is set to embark on an expedition to the Morbihan Plains, where a new series of ancient elvish tombs have recently been unearthed, including this exquisite example of prehistoric elven funerary art.}
{1033}{488c 6}
{1034}{483l According to learned professor R.I. James of the Tarantian Polytechnic Institute, who is currently on the scene, "These tombs conclusively prove that prehistoric elven society buried their dead in the ground, as I had previously theorized." Professor James discovered the tombs while searching the region for more of the Fossils which have so delighted visitors to the Polytechnic's Hall of Natural History. The unprecedented collaboration between Mssrs. James and Harriman is a unique event in the histories of their respective institutions of employment, and perhaps heralds a new era of co-operation between the two.  In any case, the curate was most effusive in praise of the kindness and good judgement of Professor James, who immediately sent for him when the nature of his discovery became clear.}

{1040}{490c GILBERT BATES MURDERED!}
{1041}{492c _ FAMED INDUSTRIALIST DEAD AT THE HANDS OF AN UNKNOWN ASSAILANT _}
{1042}{483l 	Mr. Gilbert Bates, "The Father of Industry" as he was often called, was found dead in his mansion today, apparently the victim of foul play. His assassin is still at large.}
{1043}{483l		Mr. Bates first attained national recognition as a youth when he invented the world's first steam engine. A reserved and humble man, Mr. Bates often attempted to downplay his achievements, saying he did not truly invent the steam engine, rather he "put together ideas that had been around for centuries" and often remarked upon his amazement that he was the first to do so.
	Cedric Appleby, known rival and detractor of the esteemed Mr. Bates is being considered as a possible suspect at this time. "His public denunciations of Bates and his ties to the lower degenerate classes put him at the top of our list," said the chief of police.}
{1044}{483l 	A public funerary service will be held next week. Time and location to be announced.}

{1050}{490c GILBERT BATES EXPOSED AS FRAUD!}
{1051}{492c _ BATES' JOURNAL SOLD TO PRESS, PROOF OF DWARVEN ORIGINS OF STEAM ENGINE _}
{1052}{483l 	In a development that has shocked all of the Unified Kingdom, Gilbert Bates' journal has revealed the actual origin of the steam engine - deep within the caverns of the Black Mountain Clan! 
	"I've been saying for years he is a fraud, and now I have proof," shouted Mr. Appleby, waving Bates' journal for all to see. "Thank the gods this impostor had finally been exposed!"} 
{1053}{483l		Mr. Bates could not be reached for comment, as he has left his palatial estate in Tarant for points unknown. No details of his intended destination have surfaced at this time. Public opinion still seems to be behind Gilbert Bates at this time, though, much to Mr. Appleby's consternation.
	Also involved in betraying Mr. Bates to the public is a mysterious figure, an adventurer of sorts, about which little is known.}

{1060}{490c CEDRIC APPLEBY FOUND DEAD!}
{1061}{492c _ LOCAL MISCREANT VICTIM OF FOUL PLAY _}
{1062}{483l "Inventor" Cedric Appleby was found dead in his factory late yesterday, obviously the victim of his own machinations.
	"It's fairly obvious to all that Mr. Appleby received his just reward," opined the constable on the scene, "what with him publicly berating Mr. Bates all the time, it was bound to happen sooner or later."
	The always cordial Mr. Bates had this to say about his newly deceased rival, "I am truly sorry Cedric had to come to this. Perhaps it was a disgruntled customer that brought his sad life to an end." At this point, the press and Mr. Bates had a good laugh over the whole affair.}
{1063}{483l		This reporter, for one, will miss old Cedric, as his escapades were always good for some light copy.
	Appleby, infamous for his slipshod imitations of Bates' engines, has also been linked to recent attempts to sabotage the newest Bates' engine prototype.}


{1070}{490c TRAIN WRECK AT
VERMILLION STATION!}
{1071}{492c _ DISASTER LEADS TO SEVERAL ARRESTS _}
{1072}{483l On Friday evening, the Brackenton line was derailed by magickal influences, injuring numerous bystanders and passengers.  The incident followed an attack on Councilman Edward Willoughsby and Tullan mage Perriman Smythe by local bandits.  Smythe's retaliation led to the train wreck.
Later that evening, Willoughsby and Smythe were accosted once again at the Willoughsby mansion.  Intervention by Smythe and an unidentified gentlemen led to the arrest of multiple individuals, one of them a powerful mage.  Willoughsby was uncharacteristically closed-mouthed about the affair.  
"This was a private affair, and it has been dealt with in that manner."  Smythe said he might spend some more time in the city, but plans to avoid Vermillion Station.  Said Smythe, "Dreadful affair.  I'd rather forget the whole thing." }

{1080}{490c WHYTECHURCH MURDERER STRIKES!}
{1081}{492c _ POLICE BAFFLED, ASK CITIZENS FOR HELP _}
{1082}{483l 	"Any information the citizens of Caladon could provide would be greatly appreciated," Chief Inspector Henderson told reporters earlier today. "We will use whatever means necessary to catch this heinous killer."
This is the second in what police are beginning to fear will become a string of murders if the madman responsible is not brought to justice soon.
	Details the police have shared with the public are scarce, but we do know the killer seems to have a preference for ladies of the night, as it were. "This does not mean the public can rest easy," Chief Inspector Henderson continued, "who knows when the murderer will tire of easy prey and begin seeking out our wives and daughters."}
{1083}{483l 	Any citizen with information pertaining to the case should contact the Chief Inspector immmediately.}	

{1090}{490c COUNCILMAN URGES WAR ON CALADON!}
{1091}{492c	_ THEY MUST BE STOPPED BEFORE THEY DESTROY OUR WAY OF LIFE _}
{1092}{483l 	In an impassioned, and at times violent display on the floor of the Industrial Council, Councilman Braithe called for nothing less than the unconditional surrender of Caladon, even though no formal declaration of war has been issued.
	"Were the magickally inclined in Tarant ever to try to usurp power as in the bloody mage riots of ten years past, Caladon would throw full support behind them," he screamed. "A monarchy can not be trusted, much less one that embraces both magick and technology! I say, annex Caladon now!"}
{1093}{483l This reporter could not agree more with the sentiments of Councilman Braithe. It is high time we gave those Caladonians what for! We at the Tarantian hope that you will join us in urging a recall of present chairman Willoughsby and establishing Braithe in his place.}

{1100}{490c KING OF CALADON
ASSASSINATED!}
{1101}{492c _ INCIDENT THREATENS UPCOMING NEGOTIATIONS _}
{1102}{483l	King Farad of Caladon was found dead in his chambers, the victim of a terrible attack by one or more assailants.  The assassination falls on the eve of the negotiations between the Unified Kingdom and Caladon, and seriously threatens the possibilities of Caladon's membership.  Said Prince Auguste Farad, "We've no proof yet that this was related to the negotiations, but we're still investigating.  My father will be avenged."
	Garret Blye, Captain of the Caladonian Royal Guard, had this to say.  "The fact that the murder took place in the King's Chamber's, just under our noses, points to foul play.  Many of the castle's servants are currently undergoing exhaustive, uh, questioning."
	Edward Willoughsby, the Chairman of Tarant's Industrial Council and the leading proponent of Caladon's impending membership, was unavailable for commment.}

{1110}{490c CALADONIAN MAN
BRUTALLY MUREDERED!}
{1111}{492c _ "Upstanding Citizen" survived only by his brother _}
{1112}{483l	Local gentlemen Lawrence Brummond was found dead in his apartment last evening, the victim of a brutal beating by one or more assailants.  Currently, police have no suspects in the case.
	"Its downright puzzling." said detective William Prescott. "Nothing out of the apartment was stolen, and there was plenty to steal.  From what we have pieced together, Mr. Brummond was a very upstanding citizen, from a well-respected family here in Caladon.  Somebosy took a dislike to him, and we aim to find out who and why."
	Mr. Brummond is survived only by his brother Virgil, who the police are still trying to contact.  "We're hoping he might know something about the whole affair.  We've had some trouble finding him."}

{1120}{490c OUTLANDER FINDS
STOLEN PAINTING!}
{1121}{492c _ Local Thieves Foiled by Hero _}
{1122}{483l	Local socialite Evelyn Garringsburg announced the return of her painting, the famous "Kerghan and Persephone", by an unknown gentleman of considerable resourcefulness and bravery.  The painting, found in Mulligan Bone Alley, has been missing since it was first reported stolen by the Garringsburgs last week.  
	The circumstances surrounding the paintings return are vague, but Mrs. Garringsburg had the utmost confidence in the character of the gentlemen who returned it.
	"Oh, he was just a gem!  Of course I paid him for his services, but his motivations were singularly chivalrous!  If only all the men in this dreadful city were the same."  Mr. Garringsburg was unavailable for comment.
	And to the stranger who found the paiting, this reporter takes his hat off to you, sir!  Tarant is a better place because of you.}

{1130}{490c OUTLANDER FINDS
STOLEN PAINTING!}
{1131}{492c _ Local Thieves Foiled by Heroine _}
{1132}{483l	Local socialite Evelyn Garringsburg announced the return of her painting, the famous "Kerghan and Persephone", by an unknown lady of considerable resourcefulness and bravery.  The painting, found in Mulligan Bone Alley, has been missing since it was first reported stolen by the Garringsburgs last week.  
	The circumstances surrounding the paintings return are vague, but Mrs. Garringsburg had the utmost confidence in the character of the gentlemen who returned it.
	"Oh, she was just a angel!  Of course I paid her for her services, but his motivations were completely selfless!  If only all the women in this dreadful city were the same."  Mr. Garringsburg was unavailable for comment.
	And to the stranger who found the paiting, this reporter takes his hat off to you, madam!  Tarant is a better place because of you.}

{1140}{490c IT'S A MIRACLE!
SOLE SURVIVOR SPEAKS!}
{1141}{492c _ Man Walks Away from Fiery Death _}
{1142}{483l	Local socialite Evelyn Garringsburg announced the return of her painting, the famous "Kerghan and Persephone", by an unknown lady of considerable resourcefulness and bravery.  The painting, found in Mulligan Bone Alley, has been missing since it was first reported stolen by the Garringsburgs last week.  
	The circumstances surrounding the paintings return are vague, but Mrs. Garringsburg had the utmost confidence in the character of the gentlemen who returned it.
	"Oh, she was just a angel!  Of course I paid her for her services, but his motivations were completely selfless!  If only all the women in this dreadful city were the same."  Mr. Garringsburg was unavailable for comment.
	And to the stranger who found the paiting, this reporter takes his hat off to you, madam!  Tarant is a better place because of you.}

{1150}{490c IFS ZEPHYR MIRACLE!
SURVIVOR TELLS ALL!}
{1151}{492c _ Man Walks Away from Fiery Death! _}
{1152}{483l	Greetings, fellow Tarantians!  It is I, Victor Wright, your faithful and vigilant editor, bringing you an exclusive story that only a paper of this breadth and quality could possibly do!  
	Just yesterday, our humble offices were visited by none other than the sole survivor of the IFS Zephyr blimp tragedy, and he deigned to share his account of the final minutes of that fateful voyage with me, which I now share with you!  Be warned!  This story is not for the faint of heart!  'Tis a tale so real and frightening, that I recommend it only to those with the most iron-like will and constitution!
	Upon questioning, the gentleman had this to say about his harrowing experience.  "There was a tremendous explosion...it shook the entire vessel...I didn't see anything in the air, but I heard the sound of gunfire...I also heard engines... I believe there were 2 different ones."  As impossible as it may sound, it seems the Zephyr had been attacked by some other sort of flying craft.  Upon landing, the gentleman said  that "There was wreckage also, near the blimp.  Some strange craft..."  What those craft were, the gentleman was at a loss to say.
	The fate of the Zephyr was sealed only moments after the attack.  "I am sorry to say that no one else survived the impact," the gentleman said.  He was still visibly shaken from the experience, and I stopped questioning him shortly thereafter.
	So!  Dear friends of the city!  What are we to make of this?  What were these strange craft?  Why did they attack such a noble craft as the Zephyr?  Flying bandits?  Or something even more dark and insidious?  Are we not safe even in our fair skies?  We at the Tarantian will work to bring you the truth of the matter, and I, Victor Wright, will not rest until we have done so!}

{1160}{490c IFS ZEPHYR MIRACLE!
SURVIVOR TELLS ALL!}
{1161}{492c _ Woman Walks Away from Fiery Death _}
{1162}{483l	Greetings, fellow Tarantians!  It is I, Victor Wright, your faithful and vigilant editor, bringing you an exclusive story that only a paper of this breadth and quality could possibly do!  
